course: dictionary
lesson: 8751
title: Two meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, the words around them, tells you which meaning is right. The word sett can mean a badger's home, or a stone block used to pave old streets. It is also spelt set, but here it is sett.
explain: «Badgers live in families in tunnel systems called setts.» Tunnel systems and families living together tell you a sett is a home. A sett is a set of underground tunnels and rooms dug into the earth.
explain: In an old town, a road made of setts is bumpy to ride a bike on. Here the clue is the road: a sett is a stone block, not a home. Always ask which meaning fits the sentence, then try it in place of the word to check.
fact: A sett is the home of a badger: a set of underground tunnels and rooms dug into the earth.
fact: A sett is also a rectangular stone block used to pave old streets, often wrongly called a cobble.
fact: «Badgers are protected by law in Britain, so nobody may harm a badger or block or dig into a sett.»
fact: «Real badgers live in family groups of about five or six in a sett.»
fact: Sett is also spelt set, but the two spellings do not change the meaning. Check the context to see which sense is meant.
exercise: «Badgers live in families in tunnel systems called setts.» What is a sett here? | A tunnel for trains | A type of bicycle | *A badger's underground home | A stone block used to pave a street
exercise: In an old town, the road is made of setts. What is a sett here? | *A rectangular stone block used to pave the street | A small bridge over a stream | A badger's underground home | A bag for carrying shopping
exercise: «Badgers are protected by law in Britain, so nobody may harm a badger or block or dig into a sett.» Find and copy one word that tells you a sett is a place where badgers live. | Britain | badger | law | *sett
test: «Real badgers live in family groups of about five or six in a sett.» What does sett mean? | *A home of tunnels and rooms dug into the earth | A dish for cooking | A stone path in a park | A kind of fast train
test: The bumpy road was paved with setts, so the bike shook all the way. What does sett mean? | *A stone block used to pave a street | A small pond | A badger's home | A sleeping bag
exam: Max reads, «Badgers dig special toilet pits away from the sett.» What is a sett here, and what clue tells you? | A tree, because badgers climb it | *A badger's home, because badgers dig pits away from it and live in tunnels | A bicycle, because it is bumpy | A stone block, because pits are dug in roads
